From: Armada <noreply@armada.local> [Pocknix port]
Subject: [PATCH] input: rsinput: add a per-device axis deadzone

Allow a device tree to set the same deadzone for all four joystick axes.
Keep the driver's existing defaults for devices without the property.

Pocknix port of armada 1300
`packages/kernel/patches/1300-input-rsinput-axis-deadzone.patch`
(armada-os/armada). Armada replaced ROCKNIX's global experimental Odin 3
range and deadzone defaults with per-device device-tree properties and made
the Odin 3 DTS supply `axis-range = <1024>` explicitly.

ADAPTATION
----------
Our `rsinput.c` (ROCKNIX + our delta) already reads `axis-range` from the
device tree, but it still hardcodes the four joystick deadzones to 70. This
adds the missing `axis-deadzone` property right after the `axis-range` block
in `rsinput_probe()`: when the DTS sets it, all four axes share the value;
when it does not, our 70-count default is kept. The hunk was regenerated by
hand against the exact source state this patch sees in our build (linux-7.2.9
+ 05-speedup + 10-mainline + 20-sm8750 up to 2103, 2104/2105 excluded), so it
applies with `-F0`.

NOTE (pending): armada's Odin 3 DTS supplies only `axis-range`; the
70-count `axis-deadzone` was dropped there to pass the stick value through.
Our driver default is still 70, so unless the DTS also sets
`axis-deadzone = <0>` (or we lower the default) the Odin 3 keeps a 70-count
deadzone. See the DTS/report.
---
--- a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
@@ -457,6 +457,19 @@
         }
     }
 
+    {
+        u32 deadzone;
+
+        if (!device_property_read_u32(&serdev->dev, "axis-deadzone", &deadzone)) {
+            axis_leftx_deadzone = deadzone;
+            axis_lefty_deadzone = deadzone;
+            axis_rightx_deadzone = deadzone;
+            axis_righty_deadzone = deadzone;
+
+            dev_info(&serdev->dev, "DT axis-deadzone override applied: %u\n", deadzone);
+        }
+    }
+
     drv->vdd = devm_regulator_get(&serdev->dev, "vdd");
     if (IS_ERR(drv->vdd)) {
         error = PTR_ERR(drv->vdd);
